90af006e8c113e8c0b20591f5","commit_subject":"tests: three ITs quote their SQL identifiers, so they find their tables on PostgreSQL (#8394)","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"vpnhood/VpnHood","commit_timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:32:35Z","branch":"develop","commit_sha":"6f58c56422227af8655b175d60286aa84e2b9830","github_link":"https://github.com/vpnhood/VpnHood/commit/6f58c56422227af8655b175d60286aa84e2b9830","commit_subject":"Server: the QUIC endpoint answers a burst of request headers and trailers past Kestrel's header limits with Kestrel's odds. Kestrel races its parser against its application, so one such burst gets 401, 431 or a reset, and with data after the trailers a connection error, the error more often the bigger the burst; the front answered 401 every time, which a prober repeating the burst could tell apart. It now draws, per burst, how many trailer fields it parses before choosing its reply and how much further it reads before sending it, with odds calibrated against ASP.NET Core 11.0.0-rc.3 on one host, so each answer's share falls within Kestrel's own spread. The parity suite compares every burst's shares with Kestrel's, and the cases that need one order pin it on a fixture with the server's own 401 and options (written by Astra; IP-blocking plan item 5.6.6.3)","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"repo_name":"apache/tomcat","commit_timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:32:17Z","branch":"main","commit_sha":"160e3e333f06ce291f7635592646349eecbb45a0","github_link":"https://github.com/apache/tomcat/commit/160e3e333f06ce291f7635592646349eecbb45a0","commit_subject":"Scope the tracked-list promise to closes the handle sees","ai_tool":"OpenCode"},{"repo_name":"Actual-Chat/actual-chat","commit_timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:32:15Z","branch":"dev","commit_sha":"f71e1b8fc8711c2c18af369de329de3ba52ca167","github_link":"https://github.com/Actual-Chat/actual-chat/commit/f71e1b8fc8711c2c18af369de329de3ba52ca167","commit_subject":"refactor(maui): fold MaciOS into Platforms/iOS","ai_tool":"Claude"},{"rep